shaping_report.log -- two_stage_rz   (design test_miller_ota_0908)
device-shaper, single pass after schematic-sizing CONVERGED. PDK: sky130A.

  HEADLINE:  THE Nf SWEEP DID NOT RUN.  nf IS UNMEASURED FOR THIS DESIGN.
  Every device leaves this skill with nf = 1 -- the value the netlist already
  carried. That is a CARRIED-THROUGH finger count, NOT a chosen one, and the
  two are indistinguishable by looking at the netlist. Steps 1, 3, 4 and 5
  (which need no parasitic table) did run; Step 5 changed XMP3's arrangement.

  THIS REPORT IS HAND-WRITTEN. sweep_fingers.py's --report-out could not
  produce it, because the sweep it reports on never executed.
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THE SWEEP DID NOT RUN  (a missing input -- not a failure, not a shortfall)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
sweep_fingers.py refused sky130A BY NAME, at argument parsing, before doing any
work:

    sweep_fingers.py: error: argument --pdk: invalid choice: 'sky130A'
                      (choose from )

The choice set is EMPTY -- no PDK is currently shapeable, sky130A included.
Root cause, and it is deliberate rather than broken:

  .claude/skills/parasitic-estimation/script/estimate_parasitics.py, line 96
      PDK_TABLES = {}

  with the module docstring stating that the per-process parasitic COEFFICIENT
  table is "DELIBERATELY NOT RESTORED ... Those are measured process numbers;
  inventing them would produce plausible, wrong nf decisions. It is left EMPTY
  on purpose so the caller reports the nf as never measured, rather than
  silently shaping against fabricated physics."

So this is the designed behaviour on a PDK with no measured coefficient table,
and the correct outcome is exactly what this report does: say plainly that nf
was never measured. Nothing here was estimated to fill the gap.

CONSEQUENCES, stated so nothing downstream over-reads this file:
  - There is NO per-Nf table, NO selected Nf, and NO ideal-vs-annotated drop
    measurement anywhere below. Those all belong to Step 2.
  - No spec was re-measured under annotated parasitics, so this skill reports
    NO shortfall against any spec -- not "no shortfall found", but "NOT
    MEASURED". The sizing numbers stand unchallenged because nothing challenged
    them.
  - Whether nf=1 is a good finger count for these devices is UNKNOWN. Several
    are wide (XMN4 236um total, XMN5 283.2um, XMN1/XMN2 320um each) and would
    normally be strong folding candidates.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 -- device selection (this DID run)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source: circuit_decomposition.yaml `parasitic_sensitivity`, severity=high.
Selected 7 to sweep: XMP3, XMN5, XMP2, XMN2, XMN4, XMN3, XMP4
Pinned at the netlist nf (2): XMP1=nf1, XMN1=nf1
Specs at risk per the read: Gain, PM, Power, UGBW

  NOTE (from the script): one high-severity entry names NO MOS device --
     'R0 + XC0 (rc_compensation_network), net8'
  That entry is ranked FIRST in the circuit read, and it is the one this skill
  has no lever for at all: R0 and XC0 are passives, they have no nf, and folding
  cannot touch them. The sensitivity is real and is carried to layout unchanged
  -- routing resistance in series with R0 (~1494 ohm, but only ~100 ohm of it is
  "small-resistor" margin) lands directly on the LHP zero that is holding PM at
  56.96 deg, and routing capacitance on net8 shunts Cc. Threatens PM and UGBW.

  WARNING (from the script): circuit_decomposition.yaml has
  confirmed_by_user: false. The severity ranking this selection rests on is
  therefore PROVISIONAL -- authored by schematic-agent, not yet confirmed by the
  user. Said here because the selection is only as good as that ranking.

  FINDING -- THE SELECTION SPLITS THREE TIE GROUPS. select_shape_devices.py
  does not consult `tie_groups`, so it chose XMP2 but pinned XMP1 (one
  current_mirror), and chose XMN2 but pinned XMN1 (the differential_pair).
  Sweeping one half of a matched pair to a different nf than the other would
  break exactly the matching those groups exist to protect. It did not bite
  THIS run only because the sweep never executed. If a coefficient table is
  ever added for sky130A, this must be fixed first -- either by widening the
  selection to whole tie groups or by passing --devices explicitly.

