--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  device_shaping/two_stage_rz_final_shaped_primitives.sp

  device  grouping                          w    nf  m  ->    w  nf  m   total w*m
  XMP1    in current_mirror                14.4   1  1     skipped        14.40
  XMP2    in current_mirror                14.4   1  1     skipped        14.40
  XMN1    in differential_pair               40   1  8     skipped       320.00
  XMN2    in differential_pair               40   1  8     skipped       320.00
  XMN3    in current_mirror                 47.2  1  2     skipped        94.40
  XMN4    in self_biased_reference          47.2  1  5     skipped       236.00
  XMN5    in current_mirror                 47.2  1  6     skipped       283.20
  XMP4    in self_biased_reference           1.4  1  1     skipped         1.40
  XMP3    standalone                          40  1  3  ->  120   3  1   120.00  FOLDED

ONE device folded: XMP3, the lone common_source_stage gain device.
BOTH INVARIANTS HELD for it:
    total width w*m : 40 x 3 = 120  ->  120 x 1 = 120     unchanged
    finger extent w/nf : 40 / 1 = 40  ->  120 / 3 = 40    unchanged
Only the arrangement changed: 3 rows of 1 finger -> 1 row of 3 fingers.

EIGHT devices skipped, every one because it sits inside a composed block. That
is correct and load-bearing: rewriting m on a mirror leg changes the leg ratio
the mirror is built from (5:2:6 here), and rewriting it on a diff-pair half
breaks the common-centroid split.

  *** GEOMETRY ONLY -- DO NOT SIMULATE two_stage_rz_final_shaped_primitives.sp ***
  XMP3's folded w = 120um passes sky130's widest pfet model bin (100um), so it
  matches no model card. That is expected and is why the file is separate. It
  would silently undo design-sheets-checker Step 2a's model-bin fold.
  THE SIMULABLE HAND-OFF REMAINS two_stage_rz_final_shaped.sp. Do not let the
  primitives file replace it; mixing them up yields a netlist with no model card.

  FINDING -- fold_multipliers.py MIS-PARSED THE REAL circuit_decomposition.yaml,
  SILENTLY, AND WOULD HAVE FOLDED EVERY MIRROR AND THE DIFF PAIR.
  blocked_devices() collects a pattern's devices with exactly one regex,
      re.findall(r'\{\s*ref:\s*([A-Za-z0-9_.]+)', stripped)
  so it only recognizes the mapping spelling `devices: [{ref: XMN4}, ...]`.
  circuit_decomposition.yaml uses the plain-scalar spelling the decomposition
  skill's own schema writes, `devices: [XMN4, XMN3, XMN5]`, which that regex
  never matches. All 6 patterns then parsed with EMPTY device lists, none
  reached the `>= 2 MOS` test, and the dry-run reported ALL NINE devices as
  `standalone` -- XMN1/XMN2 and XMN3/XMN4/XMN5 and XMP1/XMP2 included.
  The guard `if seen_patterns_key and not patterns` did NOT fire, because the
  pattern entries themselves parse fine; only their contents are lost. So this
  is precisely the silent failure that guard was written to prevent, arriving
  through a hole the guard does not cover.
  WORKED AROUND, not fixed: device_shaping/decomp_blocks_compat.yaml restates
  the same six patterns in the mapping spelling, and the table above is the
  result of running against it. circuit_decomposition.yaml was NOT edited.
  THE FIX belongs in fold_multipliers.py -- accept both spellings, exactly as
  its sibling parser already accepts both `- id:` and `- pattern:`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PERFORMANCE DROP ANALYSIS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOT MEASURED -- and this section is deliberately not filled with reasoning that
would look like a measurement. No Nf was swept, no parasitic was annotated, and
no spec was re-simulated under one, so there is no ideal-vs-annotated pair and
no rel_drop for any of Gain, UGBW, PM or Power.

What IS known, from the circuit read rather than from a measurement, is which
nodes would carry that drop if it were measured, in the read's own ranking:
  1. net8 / the R0-XC0 branch (PM, UGBW) -- no MOS, so no nf lever exists.
  2. vout, from XMP3 and XMN5 drains plus the MiM plate (PM, UGBW, Gain).
  3. net5, the highest-impedance node in the design (PM, UGBW, Gain).
  4. net7, the gate-only bias spine (Power, UGBW, Gain) -- carries no DC
     current, so its resistance is invisible to simulation entirely.
Devices 2-4 are folding candidates that were never folded. The honest summary
is that the design goes to layout with UGBW at +11.9% over its real bar and no
measurement of what fingers would have cost or saved it.

